Certainly! UVL (Unified Verification Language) is a formal language used for specifying hardware and software systems. However, since your request involves defining roles and constraints rather than hardware or software components, I will create a simplified UVL model that captures the essence of the given text.

Here's a possible translation:

```uvl
// Define the roles
role SecurityTeam {
    // A security team consists of the following roles
    role SecurityArchitect;
    role SecurityEngineer;
    role Manager;
    role ScrumMaster;

    // Constraints
    constraint NoSamePerson {
        SecurityArchitect!= SecurityEngineer;  // Security Architect and Security Engineer cannot be the same person
    }
}

// Define the relationship between the roles
relationship TeamRelationship {
    // Each SecurityTeam has exactly one SecurityArchitect
    one SecurityArchitect in SecurityTeam;
    // Each SecurityTeam has exactly one SecurityEngineer
    one SecurityEngineer in SecurityTeam;
    // Each SecurityTeam has exactly one Manager
    one Manager in SecurityTeam;
    // Each SecurityTeam has exactly one ScrumMaster
    one ScrumMaster in SecurityTeam;
}
```

In this UVL code:
- We define four roles: `SecurityArchitect`, `SecurityEngineer`, `Manager`, and `ScrumMaster`.
- We enforce the constraint that `SecurityArchitect` and `SecurityEngineer` cannot be the same person.
- We specify that each `SecurityTeam` must have exactly one instance of each role.

This UVL model ensures that the roles are correctly defined and the constraints are enforced as per the given text.